PART 1 – Understanding Git Branching
1. First, understand the problem
Start your class with this real-world scenario.

Imagine that a company is developing an e-commerce website.

Three developers are working on the same project.
- Developer A is developing the login functionality.
- Developer B is developing the payment functionality.
- Developer C is developing the shopping cart.
Now think:

Should all three developers modify the main project directly at the same time?

What problems might occur?
1. One developer may overwrite another developer's changes.
2. Incomplete code may enter the production application.
3. Testing new functionality may affect existing functionality.
4. Developers may accidentally introduce bugs into the working application.
We need a solution.

That solution is called Git Branching.
2. What is a Branch in Git?
A branch is an independent line of development that allows developers to work on different features without directly modifying the main development line.

Think of it like this:

You have one main project.
You want to develop three different features.
Instead of modifying the main branch directly, you create separate branches.

Example:

                    MAIN
                     |
                     |
              ---------------
              |      |      |
              |      |      |
           Login  Payment  Cart
           Branch Branch  Branch


Each developer can work independently.

After completing their work, their changes can be merged into the main branch.
Real-life analogy
Imagine a teacher preparing three different versions of an examination paper.
- Version 1: Mathematics.
- Version 2: Science.
- Version 3: Computer Science.
All versions originate from the same initial template.

Each teacher modifies their own copy.

After reviewing them, the final documents are collected.

Git branches work on a similar principle.
Important definition
A Git branch is essentially a movable reference to a commit.

This is an important technical definition.

A branch is not normally a complete independent copy of your entire project. It is a lightweight reference to a particular commit.
PART 2 – Understanding Git's Branch Architecture
3. How Git stores branches
Let's understand this technically.

Suppose we have created three commits.

A ---- B ---- C
             |
             |
            main


 Here:
- A = First commit.
- B = Second commit.
- C = Third commit.
- Main = Branch pointing to commit C.
Now suppose we create a new branch called feature-login.

A ---- B ---- C
             | \
             |  \
           main  feature-login


 Both branches initially point to the same commit.

Now switch to feature-login and create another commit.
A ---- B ---- C ---- D
             |         |
            main   feature-login


 What happened?
- Main still points to C.
- Feature-login points to D.
- The new work exists on the feature branch.
This is how Git branching works.

Teaching point: Creating a branch does not duplicate the entire project. Git creates a lightweight reference to a commit.

PART 3 – Understanding HEAD
4. What is HEAD in Git?
This is an important concept.

Explain to students:

HEAD is a special reference that identifies the currently checked-out commit. Normally, it points symbolically to the current branch.

For example:
A ---- B ---- C
             |
            main
             |
            HEAD

Here, HEAD points to the main branch, which points to commit C.

Now execute:
git switch feature-login

HEAD moves to the feature-login branch.

Example:
A ---- B ---- C
             |
             |\
             | \
           main  feature-login
                    |
                   HEAD

Important: HEAD does not necessarily point directly to a branch. In a detached HEAD state, it points directly to a commit.

We will demonstrate this later.
PART 4 – Git Branch Commands
Now start the practical demonstration.
Practical 1: Create Your First Branch
Step 1. Create a project
mkdir git-branching-lab
cd git-branching-lab

Step 2. Initialize Git
git init

Step 3. Create an HTML file
nano index.html

On Windows PowerShell:
notepad index.html

Add:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>The Fairy Bake</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
</body>
</html>

Step 4. Commit the initial version
git add .
git commit -m "Initial bakery website"

Step 5. Check existing branches
git branch

Expected output:
* main

The asterisk indicates the currently selected branch.

Depending on the Git configuration, the initial branch might be named master instead of main.
6. What is Main vs Master?
Historically, Git repositories commonly used master as the default initial branch name.

Today, main is widely used as the default branch name on GitHub and many development platforms.

Both are simply branch names.

They have no fundamental technical difference.

You can rename the current branch:
git branch -M main

This command renames the current branch to main.
Step 7. Create a new branch
git branch feature-homepage

Check:
git branch

Output:
  feature-homepage
* main

Explain:

The branch has been created, but we are still working on main.
Step 8. Switch to the new branch
git switch feature-homepage

Check:
git branch

Output:
* feature-homepage
  main

Now we are working on the feature-homepage branch.
Alternative command
Historically, developers used:
git checkout feature-homepage

Both commands can switch branches.

However, git switch is generally clearer for branch switching.
Step 9. Create and switch in one command
Instead of executing two commands:
git branch feature-about
git switch feature-about

You can execute:
git switch -c feature-about

This creates and switches to the new branch.
PART 5 – Practical: Working on Multiple Branches
Now demonstrate the most important concept.

We will develop two features independently.
Practical 2: Create Homepage and About Page Using Branches
Step 1. Switch to main
git switch main

Step 2. Create a homepage
nano index.html

Add:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
<p>Fresh bakery products.</p>

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Updated homepage"

Step 3. Create a feature branch
git switch -c feature-about

Step 4. Create about.html
nano about.html

Add:
<h1>About The Fairy Bake</h1>
<p>We provide fresh and delicious bakery products.</p>

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added about page"

Step 5. Check branch history
git log --oneline --all --graph --decorate

You will see the commit history of both branches.
Step 6. Switch back to main
git switch main

Now check:
ls

You will see the files committed on main, but about.html will not be present.

Switch back:
git switch feature-about

Now about.html will appear.
Explain why this happens.
Git updates the working directory to reflect the currently checked-out branch.

The feature branch contains the about page, while main does not yet contain that commit.

Important: Switching branches with uncommitted changes may be blocked or may carry compatible changes across. Students should commit or safely stash their work before switching.
PART 6 – Renaming and Deleting Branches
5. Renaming a branch
Suppose we created:
git branch test-feature

Now we want to rename it.
git branch -m test-feature feature-testing

If we are currently on the branch:
git branch -m feature-testing

6. Deleting a branch
Suppose we have finished working on a feature.

We no longer need the branch.

First, switch to main:
git switch main

Delete the merged branch:
git branch -d feature-about

Difference between -d and -D
Command	Meaning
git branch -d	Safely deletes a branch when Git considers it merged
git branch -D	Force-deletes a branch even if Git considers it unmerged


Be careful with -D. It can remove a branch reference to work that has not been integrated elsewhere.
Other useful commands
List local branches:
git branch

List remote-tracking branches:
git branch -r

List all branches:
git branch -a

PART 7 – Understanding Git Merge
Now we come to the most important part of today's session.
7. What is Git Merge?
Suppose Developer A has completed the login functionality.

Developer B has completed the payment functionality.

Now the company wants to combine both features into the main application.

How can we do that?

We use Git Merge.

Definition:

Git Merge combines changes from one branch into another branch.

Let's understand through a diagram.

A ---- B ---- C       main
             \
              D ---- E    feature-login

 After merging, the changes from feature-login are incorporated into main.

There are two common merge outcomes:
1. Fast-forward merge.
2. Three-way merge.
Let's understand both.
PART 8 – Fast-Forward Merge
8. What is Fast-Forward Merge?
Suppose we have:
A ---- B ---- C       main
             \
              D ---- E    feature

Actually, in a fast-forward scenario, main has not advanced since the feature branch was created. The correct starting history looks like:
A ---- B ---- C       main
             \
              D ---- E    feature

Here, main still points to C.

Feature points to E.

There is no separate new commit on main that needs to be combined.

Git can simply move the main branch reference forward to E.

This is called a Fast-Forward Merge.

After merging:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D ---- E
                              |
                             main
                              |
                            feature

Notice:

No new merge commit was required.

Git simply moved the main branch pointer forward.
Practical 3: Perform Fast-Forward Merge
Create a fresh repository for this practical.
Step 1. Create project
mkdir fast-forward-lab
cd fast-forward-lab
git init

Step 2. Create initial file
echo "Welcome to Git Training" > index.html

git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"

Step 3. Create feature branch
git switch -c feature

Step 4. Add a new feature
echo "Login functionality" > login.txt

git add .
git commit -m "Added login feature"

Step 5. Switch to main
git switch main

Step 6. Merge feature branch
git merge feature

Expected output will indicate that Git performed a fast-forward merge.
Step 7. Verify
git log --oneline --graph --all

Check files:
ls

You should now see:
index.html
login.txt

The login feature is now part of main.
PART 9 – Three-Way Merge
9. What is Three-Way Merge?
Now consider a different situation.

Developer A is working on the main branch.

Developer B creates a feature branch.

Both developers make separate commits.

Example:
          C ---- D      main
         /
A ---- B
         \
          E ---- F      feature

Now:
- Main has advanced to D.
- Feature has advanced to F.
- Both branches contain separate changes.
Git cannot simply move the main pointer forward.

It needs to combine the changes from both branches.

Git uses three commits as the basis of a three-way merge:
1. Common ancestor (B).
2. Latest commit on the current branch (D).
3. Latest commit on the branch being merged (F).
Git creates a new merge commit.

After merging:
          C ---- D --------\
         /                  \
A ---- B                    M
         \                  /
          E ---- F --------/

                           M = Merge commit

Important teaching point
Fast-forward merge simply moves a branch pointer.

Three-way merge combines divergent histories and normally creates a merge commit.
PART 10 – Practical: Three-Way Merge
Practical 4: Two Developers Working on the Same Project
We will simulate two developers.
Step 1. Create a repository
mkdir three-way-lab
cd three-way-lab
git init

Create a file:
echo "Welcome to The Fairy Bake" > index.html

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Initial website"

Step 2. Create a feature branch
git switch -c feature-menu

Create a menu file:
echo "Home | About | Contact" > menu.txt

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added navigation menu"

Step 3. Switch to main
git switch main

Make another change:
echo "Bakery business website" > description.txt

Commit:
