ls

Both files should be present:
index.html
description.txt
menu.txt

Congratulations! Students have performed a three-way merge.
PART 11 – Understanding Merge Conflicts
This is the most important real-world practical.
10. What is a Merge Conflict?
A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically reconcile changes from different branches.

For example:

Developer A changes:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

Developer B changes the same line to:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>

Now both developers have modified the same portion of the same file.

When Git attempts to merge the branches, it may not know which version should be retained.

This creates a merge conflict.

What does a conflict look like?
When Git cannot automatically merge the file, you may see:
<<<<<<< HEAD
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
=======
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>
>>>>>>> feature

Explain each marker:
Marker	Meaning
<<<<<<< HEAD	Beginning of the current branch's conflicting content
=======	Separator between the two versions
>>>>>>> feature	End of the incoming branch's conflicting content


Important: These markers are inserted by Git to help the developer resolve the conflict. They are not valid HTML and must be removed before completing the merge.
PART 12 – Practical: Resolve a Merge Conflict
Practical 5: Real-World Conflict Resolution
We will simulate two developers modifying the same file.
Step 1. Create a new repository
mkdir merge-conflict-lab
cd merge-conflict-lab
git init

Step 2. Create an initial file
echo "<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>" > index.html

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Initial homepage"

Step 3. Create Developer A's branch
git switch -c developer-A

Modify the file:
nano index.html

Change it to:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake - Developer A</h1>

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Updated homepage by Developer A"

Step 4. Switch to main
git switch main

Step 5. Create Developer B's branch
git switch -c developer-B

Modify the same file:
nano index.html

Change it to:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake - Developer B</h1>

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Updated homepage by Developer B"

Step 6. Merge Developer A into main
git switch main
git merge developer-A

This should merge successfully.
Step 7. Merge Developer B into main
git merge developer-B

Now Git should report a merge conflict because both branches modified the same line differently.

Check:
git status

Expected observation:
both modified: index.html

Step 8. Open the conflicting file
nano index.html

You should see conflict markers similar to:
<<<<<<< HEAD
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake - Developer A</h1>
=======
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake - Developer B</h1>
>>>>>>> developer-B

Step 9. Resolve the conflict manually
Tell students that they are now the project manager.

They must decide which changes to keep.

For example, they can combine both contributions:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

<p>Homepage updated by Developer A and Developer B.</p>

Save the file.
Step 10. Stage the resolved file
git add index.html

Step 11. Complete the merge
git commit -m "Resolved homepage merge conflict"

Step 12. Verify
git status
git log --oneline --graph --all

The merge is complete.

Explain to students:

Git does not decide which developer is correct. Developers must resolve conflicting changes according to project requirements.
What if we want to cancel a merge?
If a merge conflict occurs and we decide not to continue:
git merge --abort

This attempts to restore the pre-merge state.
PART 13 – Detached HEAD State
11. What is Detached HEAD?
This is an intermediate-to-advanced concept.

Normally:
HEAD → main → Commit C

HEAD points to the current branch.

But suppose we check out a specific commit directly.
git checkout <commit-id>

Now:
HEAD → Commit B

main → Commit C

HEAD is no longer attached to a branch.

This is called Detached HEAD State.
Why does it happen?
Common reasons:
- Checking out an old commit.
- Inspecting an earlier project version.
- Testing an earlier implementation.
- Checking out a tag.
Practical demonstration
First:
git log --oneline

Copy an older commit ID.

Now execute:
git checkout <old-commit-id>

Git will display a detached HEAD warning.

Check:
git status

You are now inspecting an older commit.
How to return to the branch?
git switch main

What if we want to save changes made in Detached HEAD?
Suppose you make a modification and commit it while detached.

If you simply switch away, that commit may eventually become unreachable.

The safer approach is to create a branch:
git switch -c recovery-branch

Now your work has a branch reference.

Important teaching point: Detached HEAD is not an error. It is simply a state in which HEAD points directly to a commit rather than following a branch.
PART 14 – Git Merge vs Git Rebase
This is an important interview and industry concept.
12. What is Git Rebase?
Git Rebase is used to replay commits from one branch onto another base commit.

It helps create a more linear project history.

Consider:
          C ---- D       main
         /
A ---- B
         \
          E ---- F       feature

The feature branch has commits E and F.

Meanwhile, main has advanced to D.

We want to bring the feature branch up to date with main.

Using rebase:
git switch feature
git rebase main

Git takes the feature commits and replays them on top of the latest main commit.

Conceptually:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D ---- E' ---- F'

Notice:
- E and F are recreated as E' and F'.
- Their commit IDs change.
- The project history becomes linear.
Practical demonstration
Create a fresh repository.
mkdir rebase-lab
cd rebase-lab
git init

Create an initial file:
echo "Version 1" > app.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"

Create a feature branch:
git switch -c feature

Add a feature:
echo "Feature implementation" >> app.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Added feature"

Switch to main:
git switch main

Add another change:
echo "Main branch update" > main.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Updated main branch"

Switch back to feature:
git switch feature

Rebase:
git rebase main

Check:
git log --oneline --graph --all

Now feature commits have been replayed on top of main.

If conflicts occur during rebase:
git status

Resolve the conflicting files, then:
git add <filename>
git rebase --continue

To cancel:
git rebase --abort

Important: Avoid rebasing commits that have already been shared with other developers unless the team has explicitly agreed to rewrite that history.

PART 15 – Git Merge vs Git Rebase
Students must understand this comparison.

| Feature | Git Merge | Git Rebase |
| Purpose | Combine branch histories | Replay commits on another base |
| History | Can preserve branching structure | Creates a linear history |
| Merge commit | Often created for divergent branches | Normally not created |
| Existing commit IDs | Preserved | Replayed commits receive new IDs |
| Conflict handling | Resolve during merge | Resolve during replay |
| Shared branches | Generally suitable | Requires caution |
| Main advantage | Preserves historical context | Cleaner linear history |


Which one should developers use?
Use Merge when:
- You want to preserve the history of how branches were integrated.
- You are integrating shared feature branches.
- You do not want to rewrite existing commit history.
Use Rebase when:
- You want to update a private feature branch with the latest main branch.
- You want a linear commit history.
- The commits have not yet been shared with others.
A common professional workflow is:
Developer creates feature branch
          |
          v
Works on feature
          |
          v
Updates feature using rebase
          |
          v
Pushes feature branch
          |
          v
Creates Pull Request
          |
          v
Code Review
          |
          v
Merge into main

